# -----------------------------------------------
# Understanding "/" (forward slash) in .gitignore
#
# "/" at the START of a rule — controls WHERE
# The rule applies only at the project root level.
#
# Example: /build/
# android-cicd/build/        ← ignored ✅
# android-cicd/app/build/    ← NOT ignored ❌
#
# "/" at the END of a rule — controls WHAT
# The rule applies only to directories, not files.
#
# Example: build/
# android-cicd/build/        ← ignored ✅ (folder)
# android-cicd/app/build/    ← ignored ✅ (folder)
# android-cicd/build         ← NOT ignored ❌ (file)
#
# No "/" at START or END — matches both files and folders
# The rule applies everywhere in the project.
#
# Example: build
# android-cicd/build/        ← ignored ✅ (folder)
# android-cicd/app/build/    ← ignored ✅ (folder)
# android-cicd/build         ← ignored ✅ (file)
#
# Quick Summary of build ignore rules
#
# Rule      | What it ignores
# ----------|------------------------------------
# /build/   | Only the root build folder
# build/    | Any build folder anywhere
# build     | Files or folders named "build" anywhere
# -----------------------------------------------

# -----------------------------------------------
# Built Application Files
#
# What is * (wildcard)?
# * means "match anything" — it does not matter what
# the file is named — if it ends with the extension
# it will be ignored
#
# *.apk — Android Package Kit file
#          This is the actual installable app file
#          that gets installed on Android devices
#          example: app-release.apk, app-debug.apk
#
# *.aab — Android App Bundle file
#          This is the file you upload to Google Play Console
#          Google Play uses this to generate optimized APKs
#          for each device automatically
#          example: app-release.aab, app-debug.aab
#
# *.dex — Dalvik Executable file
#          This is the compiled bytecode of your
#          Kotlin/Java code that runs on Android devices
#          example: classes.dex, classes2.dex
#
# When are these files generated?
#
# Situation 1 — When you click Run in Android Studio
#          Android Studio automatically compiles your
#          code and generates all these files every
#          time you click the Run button
#
# Situation 2 — When you build manually via Build menu
#          Build → Assemble 'app' Run Configuration
#               generates debug APK for current
#               run configuration
#          Build → Compile Selected Modules
#               compiles only the selected module
#               generates .class and .dex files
#          Build → Compile All Sources
#               compiles all source files in project
#               generates .class and .dex files
#          Build → Assemble Selected Modules
#               generates APK for selected modules only
#          Build → Assemble Project
#               generates APK for entire project
#               output in app/build/outputs/apk/
#          Build → Generate App Bundles or APKs
#               generates either .aab or .apk file
#               output in app/build/outputs/bundle/
#               or app/build/outputs/apk/
#          Build → Generate Signed App Bundle or APK
#               generates signed .aab or .apk file
#               signed with your keystore credentials
#               output in app/release/
#
# Situation 3 — When GitHub Actions runs your CI/CD workflow
#          GitHub Actions runs ./gradlew bundleRelease
#          and generates .class, .dex and .aab files
#          at app/build/outputs/bundle/release/app-release.aab
#          on the GitHub Actions machine — not on your computer
#
# Why ignore all of these?
# All of these files are generated automatically
# every time you run or build your app — they are
# output files not source files. Any developer can
# regenerate them instantly just by clicking Run
# in Android Studio or by running ./gradlew build
# Pushing them only wastes storage space and causes
# unnecessary conflicts between different machines
# -----------------------------------------------
*.apk
*.aab
*.dex

# -----------------------------------------------
# Java / Kotlin Class Files
#
# *.class — Compiled Java or Kotlin bytecode file
#            When you write code in Kotlin or Java
#            the compiler converts it into .class files
#            which the JVM can understand and execute
#            In your project these would be generated
#            inside app/build/intermediates/ and
#            app/build/kotlin/ folders
#            example: MainActivity.class
#
# Why ignore these?
# These are intermediate compiled files generated
# automatically during every build — they are not
# your source code. Your actual source code lives in
# app/src/main/java/com/arbutusinfotech/upkeep/
# Any developer can regenerate .class files by
# building the project
# -----------------------------------------------
*.class

# -----------------------------------------------
# Generated Build Directories
#
# build/ — Gradle creates a build/ folder inside
#           every module automatically during build
#           Git treats this rule as recursive so
#           one rule covers all of these:
#           build/         — root level
#           app/build/     — app module
#           library/build/ — library module
#           any other module build/ folder
#
# When is this created?
#           Every time you click Run in Android Studio
#           Every time you go to Build → Assemble Project
#           Every time GitHub Actions runs ./gradlew bundleRelease
#           Gradle recreates it fresh every single time
#
# Why ignore this?
#           Auto generated on every build
#           Can be hundreds of MB in size
#           Machine specific output files
#           Any developer can regenerate instantly
#           by clicking Run in Android Studio
# -----------------------------------------------
build/

# -----------------------------------------------
# Gradle Cache
#
# .gradle/ — Gradle stores downloaded dependencies
#             and build metadata in this directory
#             to speed up future builds
#             This folder is machine specific and
#             contains cached files and local paths
#             that are only relevant to the current
#             development environment
#
# What about gradle/wrapper/ files?
# The gradle/wrapper/ directory is different from
# .gradle/ and should NOT be ignored. Git already
# tracks these files automatically:
#   gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar
#   gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.properties
#   gradlew
#   gradlew.bat
# These files allow developers and CI systems
# such as GitHub Actions to run Gradle without
# installing it manually — they must remain in Git
#
# .gradle-cache/ — sometimes appears in CI environments
#                   like GitHub Actions when Gradle
#                   caches dependencies between runs
#                   Machine specific — ignore this too
#
# Why ignore .gradle/?
# It is an auto generated Gradle cache that:
# — is specific to each machine
# — changes frequently during development
# — is recreated automatically when the project builds
# -----------------------------------------------
.gradle/
.gradle-cache/

# -----------------------------------------------
# Local Configuration File
#
# local.properties — automatically created by
#                     Android Studio and contains:
#                     sdk.dir=/Users/manishjain/Library/Android/sdk
#                     This path only works on the
#                     machine where it was created
#                     On your machine the path starts
#                     with /Users/manishjain/ — on
#                     any other machine this path
#                     does not exist and will cause
#                     Android Studio to show errors
#
# Why ignore this?
# Android Studio recreates this file automatically
# for each developer with their own correct SDK path
# There is never any reason to push this file
# -----------------------------------------------
local.properties

# -----------------------------------------------
# Keystore Folder and Signing Files
#
# Your project has a keystore/ folder at the root
# level containing two files:
#
# keystore/upkeep_keystore.jks — your actual keystore
#                                  file containing your
#                                  app signing credentials
#                                  NEVER push this file
#
# keystore/keystore.properties — contains your keystore
#                                  passwords in plain text
#                                  including store password
#                                  key alias and key password
#                                  NEVER push this file
#
# keystore/ — ignoring the entire folder automatically
#              covers both files inside it
#              No need to list them separately
#
# *.jks — safety net rule
#          If someone accidentally places a .jks file
#          outside the keystore/ folder — for example
#          directly in the project root or in app/ —
#          this rule will still catch and ignore it
#
# Why is this critical?
# upkeep_keystore.jks contains your app signing
# credentials. If pushed to a public repository
# anyone can download and misuse it to sign fake
# versions of your app. keystore.properties contains
# your passwords in plain text — equally dangerous.
# These credentials should be stored securely
# for example using CI/CD secrets such as
# GitHub Secrets — covered in Step 4
# -----------------------------------------------
keystore/
*.jks

# -----------------------------------------------
# Android Studio and IntelliJ Settings
#
# .idea/ — Located at the root level of the project
#           Android Studio stores project configuration
#           here including editor preferences
#           run configurations, code style settings
#           and debugger settings
#           These files change frequently during normal
#           development — opening files, changing settings
#           so they should not be tracked in Git
#           Ignoring the entire .idea/ folder
#           automatically covers everything inside it
#           No need to list individual files or
#           subfolders separately
#
# *.iml — IntelliJ Module files
#          Automatically regenerated by Android Studio
#          when the project is opened
#          example: android-cicd.iml, app.iml
#
# Why ignore these?
# These files are specific to each developer's
# machine and Android Studio installation
# Android Studio automatically recreates the
# correct configuration when the project is opened
# -----------------------------------------------
*.iml
.idea/

# -----------------------------------------------
# Kotlin Cache
#
# .kotlin/ — created by the Kotlin compiler to
#             store incremental compilation cache
#             This speeds up future builds by only
#             recompiling files that have changed
#             Completely machine specific — has no
#             value on any other machine
#             Already visible in your project structure
#
# Why ignore this?
# Auto generated by Kotlin compiler on every build
# Machine specific — changes constantly
# -----------------------------------------------
.kotlin/

# -----------------------------------------------
# External Native Build
#
# .externalNativeBuild/ — generated when your project
#                          uses C/C++ code via Android NDK
#                          Your current project does not
#                          use native code but this rule
#                          is here as a safety net in case
#                          you add native code later
#
# .cxx/ — CMake build folder for C/C++ native code
#          Same as above — safety net for future use
#
# Why ignore these?
# Auto generated during native builds
# Machine specific and regenerated automatically
# -----------------------------------------------
.externalNativeBuild/
.cxx/

# -----------------------------------------------
# Android Studio Captures
#
# captures/ — Android Studio saves screenshots and
#              screen recordings here when you use
#              the Screen Capture tool during debugging
#              Personal debugging files with no value
#              to other developers or to GitHub Actions
#
# Why ignore this?
# Personal debugging screenshots and recordings
# Can be large files — especially screen recordings
# No relevance to the project source code
# -----------------------------------------------
captures/

# -----------------------------------------------
# Navigation Editor Temp Files
#
# .navigation/ — Android Studio Navigation editor
#                 creates temporary files here when
#                 you use the visual navigation graph
#                 editor to design your app navigation
#                 These are regenerated automatically
#                 every time you open the editor
#
# Why ignore this?
# Auto generated temporary files that change
# constantly — no reason to push them
# -----------------------------------------------
.navigation/

# -----------------------------------------------
# Log Files
#
# *.log — any file ending with .log
#          Generated during app runtime and during
#          Gradle build processes
#          example: debug.log, build.log, error.log
#          Change constantly during development
#          Specific to your current session only
#
# Why ignore these?
# Change on every run — not part of source code
# Can become very large over time
# Specific to your machine and session
# -----------------------------------------------
*.log

# -----------------------------------------------
# macOS System Files
#
# .DS_Store — macOS creates this hidden file in
#              every folder you open in Finder
#              Stores display preferences for folders
#              Completely invisible to you but Git
#              can see it and will try to track it
#              Meaningless on Windows or Linux
#
# Why ignore this?
# Auto generated by macOS in every folder
# Changes constantly just from opening Finder
# Has zero relevance to your Android project
# -----------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride

# -----------------------------------------------
# Windows System Files
#
# Thumbs.db  — Windows thumbnail cache file
#               auto generated in folders with images
# ehthumbs.db — Windows Media Center thumbnail cache
# Desktop.ini — Windows folder customization file
#
# Why ignore these?
# Windows specific system files — auto generated
# No relevance to Android project or other OS
# -----------------------------------------------
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini